  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $49,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 7$ and $\\pm 49.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{49}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{49}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{49},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
